ty percent. The Democratic-tilted national environment keeps this short of Safe, but nothing in the local coverage suggests Crosson has made the race genuinely competitive.", "watch": "Any polling showing Crosson within single digits, or further national deterioration for Republicans, would move this toward Tossup territory.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Tony Wied (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Tony Wied, a first-term Republican incumbent, is seeking re-election in northeastern Wisconsin's 8th District, a seat that leans Republican by about eight points. His challenger, Democrat Rick Crosson, has run a visible campaign drawing on his personal ties to the military, and the two met in an October debate. Wied holds a large financial advantage, raising about $1.35 million to Crosson's roughly $170,000.\n\nNo public polls of the race are available, so the outlook rests on the district's partisan lean, the incumbent's fundraising edge, and expert ratings, which uniformly favor Wied, most calling the seat solidly or safely Republican. Prediction markets price Wied's chances at roughly ninety percent. A Democratic-leaning national environment could narrow the margin, but Crosson would need a substantial wave to overcome the district's fundamentals.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0582, "margin": -10.2}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.052, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.066, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0699, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.033, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0472, "margin": -11.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.0442, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.0408, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.0357, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.0325, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.0408, "